description: Use Bun instead of Node.js, npm, pnpm, or vite.
globs: "*.ts, *.tsx, *.html, *.css, *.js, *.jsx, package.json"
alwaysApply: false
---

Default to using Bun instead of Node.js.

- Use `bun <file>` instead of `node <file>` or `ts-node <file>`
- Use `bun test` instead of `jest` or `vitest`
- Use `bun build <file.html|file.ts|file.css>` instead of `webpack` or `esbuild`
- Use `bun install` instead of `npm install` or `yarn install` or `pnpm install`
- Use `bun run <script>` instead of `npm run <script>` or `yarn run <script>` or `pnpm run <script>`
- Bun automatically loads .env, so don't use dotenv.

## APIs

- `Bun.serve()` supports WebSockets, HTTPS, and routes. Don't use `express`.
- `bun:sqlite` for SQLite. Don't use `better-sqlite3`.
- `Bun.redis` for Redis. Don't use `ioredis`.
- `Bun.sql` for Postgres. Don't use `pg` or `postgres.js`.
- `WebSocket` is built-in. Don't use `ws`.
- Prefer `Bun.file` over `node:fs`'s readFile/writeFile
- Bun.$`ls` instead of execa.

## Testing

Use `bun test` to run tests.

```ts#index.test.ts
import { test, expect } from "bun:test";

test("hello world", () => {
  expect(1).toBe(1);
});
```

## Frontend

Use HTML imports with `Bun.serve()`. Don't use `vite`. HTML imports fully support React, CSS, Tailwind.

Server:

```ts#index.ts
import index from "./index.html"

Bun.serve({
  routes: {
    "/": index,
    "/api/users/:id": {
      GET: (req) => {
        return new Response(JSON.stringify({ id: req.params.id }));
      },
    },
  },
  // optional websocket support
  websocket: {
    open: (ws) => {
      ws.send("Hello, world!");
    },
    message: (ws, message) => {
      ws.send(message);
    },
    close: (ws) => {
      // handle close
    }
  },
  development: {
    hmr: true,
    console: true,
  }
})
```

HTML files can import .tsx, .jsx or .js files directly and Bun's bundler will transpile & bundle automatically. `<link>` tags can point to stylesheets and Bun's CSS bundler will bundle.

```html#index.html
<html>
  <body>
    <h1>Hello, world!</h1>
    <script type="module" src="./frontend.tsx"></script>
  </body>
</html>
```

With the following `frontend.tsx`:

```tsx#frontend.tsx
import React from "react";

// import .css files directly and it works
import './index.css';

import { createRoot } from "react-dom/client";

const root = createRoot(document.body);

export default function Frontend() {
  return <h1>Hello, world!</h1>;
}

root.render(<Frontend />);
```

Then, run index.ts

```sh
bun --hot ./index.ts
```

For more information, read the Bun API docs in `node_modules/bun-types/docs/**.md`.

## Project Context: Retail Arbitrage (VALUE_ALPHA)

This project automates a retail-arbitrage loop across marketplaces, with Shopify merchants as the buy/source side and eBay as the primary sell side.

### High-Level Objective

- Keep an up-to-date source-merchant catalog mirror.
- List high-priority inventory to eBay.
- Unlist eBay items when source inventory is sold out.
- React to eBay sold events by triggering automated source-merchant checkout.

### Marketplace Scope

- `SOURCE MERCHANTS`: Shopify-backed catalogue feeds (multi-merchant)
- `SELL MARKETPLACES`: eBay (bulk listing API), Depop (bulk API support in progress)

### Current State

- `catalog` table schema exists and has been applied to production.
- Core remaining work is catalog sync deltas, listing automation, and checkout orchestration.

### Agent Priorities (Implementation Order)

1. Merchant scrape logic + API to fetch full updated catalog.
2. Delta detection:
   - `SOLD`: identify source items that must be unlisted from eBay.
   - `NEW`: identify source items that must be queued/listed on eBay.
3. Item ranking logic for eBay posting priority.
4. eBay posting pipeline and subscription setup for app/user accounts.
5. eBay sold webhook listener.
6. Source-merchant checkout agent steps (customer details, virtual card, confirmation, tracking).
7. Depop bulk API fixes.

### Catalog Data Contract

Treat `catalog` as the source-of-truth sync table keyed by `handle`. Preserve and use lifecycle fields:

- Availability/sale: `available`, `is_sold`, `sold_on`, `last_snapshot`
- eBay sync: `ebay_listing_id`, `ebay_status`, `listed_on`, `unlisted_on`, `last_ebay_error`
- payload traceability: `raw_payload`

Accepted `ebay_status` values:

- `not_listed`
- `queued`
- `listed`
- `pending_unlist`
- `unlist_failed`
- `list_failed`

### Agent Guidance

- Prefer minimal, contained changes tied directly to sell/buy pipeline milestones.
- Avoid introducing new abstractions unless needed for a specific milestone.
- Keep logic idempotent for catalog sync and listing/unlisting operations.

### eBay Sync Interrupt Semantics

- Treat eBay sync `Ctrl+C` behavior as two-stage shutdown:
  - First interrupt: graceful drain (stop new queue intake, finish in-flight listing attempts, then exit).
  - Second interrupt: forced exit (`130`).
- Rapid duplicate signal events are debounced to avoid accidental forced exit from a single keypress.
- If no jobs are in-flight, first-interrupt graceful drain can finish immediately.

### Python Workspace Structure (Current)

- Python listing helpers now live under `apps/` as:
  - `apps/ebay_add_listing`
  - `apps/ebay_remove_listing`
  - `apps/upload_ebay_aspects`
- Use the root `uv` workspace (global approach), not per-app virtualenvs.
- Run Python commands from repo root via `uv run --package <package_name> ...`.
